<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Rogaland</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 880 CE (approx.) · capital Avaldsnes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">West Norwegian kingdom whose seat at Avaldsnes guarded the Karmsund strait, the 'north way' that gave Norway its name.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rogaland, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavanger">Stavanger</a>, was home to a powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> elite. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaldsnes">Avaldsnes</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmoy">Karmøy</a>, great mounds and a ship burial of the late 8th century mark a seat of kings who controlled shipping through the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmsund">Karmsund</a> strait along the coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Control of the sea route allowed the rulers to tax or plunder traffic passing north and south. The district had good farmland on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaeren">Jæren</a> and close links across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Its kings, known only from the sagas, were rivals of the kings of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vestfold">Vestfold</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hafrsfjord">Battle of Hafrsfjord</a>, traditionally dated around 872, was fought in Rogaland, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> defeated the western kings. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaldsnes">Avaldsnes</a> one of his royal residences, and Rogaland became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rogaland.jpg" alt="Rogaland" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Giuseppe Milo · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverd_I_Fjell_Stavanger_Norway_Landscape_Travel_Photography_(120169943).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Avaldsnes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Rogaland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=rogaland" aria-label="Rogaland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4801 193 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rogaland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5121 449L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5121 449h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5121 449h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=rogaland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
